content.theory:
In JavaScript, **functions are values**. You can store a function in a variable, pass it to another function, and return it from a function. A function that takes or returns another function is called a **higher-order function**, and the function passed in is a **callback**. This idea sits at the heart of modern JS: event handlers, timers, and array methods are all built on it.

### Functions as values
```js
const greet = () => "Hello!";
console.log(greet());   // Hello!
```

### Callbacks with setTimeout
```js
setTimeout(() => {
  console.log("3 seconds passed");
}, 3000);
```
The arrow function is a callback — code that runs later, when the timer fires.

### map / filter / reduce — the big three
```js
const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const doubled = nums.map(n => n * 2);        // [2, 4, 6, 8]
const evens = nums.filter(n => n % 2 === 0); // [2, 4]
const sum = nums.reduce((total, n) => total + n, 0); // 10
```
`map` transforms every item, `filter` keeps items that pass a test, and `reduce` boils the array down to a single value. Each one takes a callback.

### Returning functions — closures
```js
function makeAdder(x) {
  return (y) => x + y;
}
const add5 = makeAdder(5);
console.log(add5(3));   // 8
```
`add5` is a function returned by `makeAdder`. It remembers the `x` it was created with — that memory is called a **closure**, and it is the reason returning functions is so powerful.

### Why this matters
Modern frameworks like React lean on higher-order patterns constantly: components take props (data), handlers are callbacks, and lists are rendered with `.map()`. Master the callback habit — always ask "what function am I passing, and when will it be called?" A common mistake is calling the function instead of passing it: `setTimeout(doThing(), 1000)` runs `doThing` immediately. Pass the reference, not the result: `setTimeout(doThing, 1000)`.

